arm64: dts: h713: the IR receiver at sunxi-cir's default 8 MHz, and a sleep state for PL9

clock-frequency = <24000000> made sunxi-cir compute rx_resolution = 1000000 / (24000000 / 64)
= 2 us in integer arithmetic for a real 2.667 us sample, so every duration reached rc-core at
0.75 of its length and NEC never decoded (measurement 02.10.2026: leader 6752/3348 us). The
clock itself was right: mux 01 is dcxo24M on the sun50iw12 R_CCU (vendor r_ir_parents
0xc0ea23cc = {osc32k, dcxo24M}, 1-bit mux at bit 24). Without the property the driver asks for
8 MHz = hosc / 3 and the resolution is an exact 8 us. The sleep state is stock's (s_cir0@1,
gpio_in); sunxi-cir passes through it to re-apply PL9 after the ARISC start. FB-REMOTEb.
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
@@ -1867,6 +1867,14 @@
 				input-enable;
 			};
 
+			/* Stock's sleep state for PL9 (s_cir0@1). sunxi-cir
+			 * passes through it to re-apply r_ir_rx_pin after the
+			 * ARISC firmware's start has disabled PL8/PL9. */
+			r_ir_rx_sleep_pin: r-ir-rx-sleep-pin {
+				pins = "PL9";
+				function = "gpio_in";
+			};
+
 			s_pwm1_pin: s-pwm1-pin {
 				pins = "PL7";
 				function = "s_pwm1";
@@ -1897,10 +1905,16 @@
 			clocks = <&r_ccu CLK_BUS_R_IR_RX>,
 				 <&r_ccu CLK_R_IR_RX>;
 			clock-names = "apb", "ir";
-			clock-frequency = <24000000>;
+			/*
+			 * No clock-frequency: the driver's default 8 MHz is
+			 * hosc / 3 and gives exactly 8 us per sample. At 24 MHz
+			 * sunxi-cir's integer rx_resolution is 2 us for a real
+			 * 2.667 us - every duration came out 0.75 of NEC.
+			 */
 			resets = <&r_ccu RST_BUS_R_IR_RX>;
-			pinctrl-names = "default";
+			pinctrl-names = "default", "sleep";
 			pinctrl-0 = <&r_ir_rx_pin>;
+			pinctrl-1 = <&r_ir_rx_sleep_pin>;
 			status = "okay";
 		};
 
